[FINAL-TEXT] I have all the evidence needed. Here is the complete diagnosis:

---

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The affected subsystem is the RIP routing daemon (`ripd`) on router0, the Location A border router. The file `/etc/frr/ripd.conf` is present but has been emptied to 0 bytes. As a result, when FRR started, `ripd` had no `router rip` process block and no `network` statements, so it either crashed immediately or exited, leaving the `frr.service` in a `failed` state. Only zebra, mgmtd, and staticd are running on router0; there is no `ripd` process.

Without `ripd`, router0 does not exchange RIP updates with its neighbours (router1 on the 10.16.1.0/24 WAN link, and router5 on the 172.20.1.0/24 link). This has two consequences: (1) router0 never learns any remote-network routes, so its routing table contains only its own directly-connected subnets (172.16.10.0/24, 172.16.20.0/24, 192.168.1.0/24, 172.20.1.0/24, 10.16.1.0/24) and two static kernel routes, with no path to Location B or Location C prefixes; (2) router0 does not advertise Location A subnets into the RIP domain (though routers 1 and 5 still show these, likely due to cached holddowns from the brief moment the lab initialised).

When PC4 (172.16.10.4) or Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) sends a packet to an outside host, the packet reaches router0's VLAN sub-interface (confirmed reachable), router0 looks up the destination, finds no matching route, and returns an ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — exactly what was observed in the ping from pc4 to pc11.

**Root cause**: `/etc/frr/ripd.conf` was wiped (set to 0 bytes) on router0, preventing the RIP daemon from loading any configuration and causing the FRR service to fail.

**Fix**: Restore `/etc/frr/ripd.conf` on router0 with a valid RIP configuration — at minimum `router rip` with `version 2` and `network` statements covering all connected interfaces (e.g., `network 10.0.0.0/8`, `network 172.16.0.0/16`, `network 172.20.0.0/16`, `network 192.168.0.0/16`), matching the style used by router1 and router5 — then run `systemctl restart frr`.
=== END ===